          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I have been getting heartburn most evenings.",
          "answer": "**If you have chest pain or pressure, shortness of breath, sweating, nausea, or pain spreading to your jaw, neck, arm, or back, call emergency services now.** Heartburn and heart-related chest pain can feel alike, and the guidance says a history of reflux does not rule out a heart cause (Chest pain that may indicate a heart attack). Antacid relief does not rule it out either.\n\nBased on what you've described, the check found no emergency pattern. That is not the same as being cleared. Heartburn that comes back most evenings is common, and the reference material says the pattern matters: heartburn that is worse after meals, when bending forward, or when lying down fits the typical picture (Gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD)). Large or late meals, alcohol, smoking, and excess weight can all contribute.\n\nThe guideline on GERD says that for adults with typical heartburn and no warning signs, a clinician may start with a trial of acid-reducing medicine before arranging tests. Whether that suits you is a decision for a clinician (GERD: empiric initial management and the alarm features that change it).\n\nThe material I searched did not cover what is causing your heartburn in your case, which treatments suit you, or which warning signs would apply to you specifically. Those questions are for a doctor or pharmacist. See a clinician soon if the heartburn keeps happening, gets worse, or does not respond to over-the-counter remedies. Also see one promptly if you have trouble swallowing, unexplained weight loss, vomiting blood, or black stools.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
