          "turn_index": 1,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Would changing when I eat help?",
          "answer": "Possibly, yes. The lifestyle reference on eating with reflux says meal size and meal timing carry more weight than food choice. A large meal can raise pressure on the valve at the top of the stomach, so smaller portions may cause fewer symptoms. Lying down soon after eating is also a factor, because a full stomach has less to keep its contents down. The reference describes leaving several hours between your last meal and lying down as the change most often recommended, and says it can help most when symptoms mainly happen at night (Eating with reflux: meal size, timing, and finding individual triggers).\n\nThe GERD guideline gives the same advice: avoid meals within two to three hours of lying down, and raise the head of the bed if you have night-time symptoms. It also suggests avoiding specific trigger foods you have identified yourself, rather than cutting out whole food groups (GERD: empiric initial management and the alarm features that change it).\n\nThe material did not say how much change to expect for you, or whether these steps would be enough on their own. A clinician or pharmacist can help you decide. Keep the heartburn warnings from earlier in mind. Get urgent help for chest pain or pressure, and see a clinician promptly for trouble swallowing, unexplained weight loss, vomiting blood, or black stools.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
